# grok-tua / tok-tua environment configuration
#
# Copy this file to .env and fill in the values for your setup:
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# bin/grok-tua and bin/tok-tua source this repo's own .env at launch (if
# present). Nothing here is required to get a working local dashboard —
# every var below has a sane localhost default baked into the code; .env is
# only needed to point at a real gateway key or a second machine on your LAN.

# --- Gateway auth -----------------------------------------------------------
# API key for your Headroom/LiteLLM gateway. Used as the OPENAI_API_KEY /
# ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN fallback for CLI panes launched by tok-tua.
# LITELLM_MASTER_KEY=sk-...

# --- Local gateway (defaults shown; usually no need to set these) ----------
# HEADROOM_BASE=http://127.0.0.1:8787
# LITELLM_BASE=http://127.0.0.1:4000
# TURNSTONE_BASE=http://127.0.0.1:8090

# --- Optional LAN fallback ---------------------------------------------------
# If you run Headroom/LiteLLM on a second machine on your network (a NAS, a
# home server, a GPU box, etc.), set its IP here. grok-tua/tok-tua will only
# fall back to it if the local gateway is unreachable.
# NAS_HOST_IP=192.168.1.X
# LITELLM_BASE_FALLBACK=http://192.168.1.X:4000

# Optional: a second LAN host preferred for GPU-heavy routing (Turnstone UI,
# Grafana, Open WebUI candidate URLs).
# GPU_HOST_IP=192.168.1.X

# --- Optional: remote-host container healing over SSH -----------------------
# grok-tua's launch smoke-test can try restarting known Docker container
# names on a remote host over SSH when the local stack is down. Off unless
# both of these are set.
# NAS_HOST_SSH=user@192.168.1.X
# GROK_TUA_SMOKE_NAS_HOST=1

# --- Optional: local docker-compose healing ---------------------------------
# Directory containing your own gateway's docker-compose.yml, if you want
# grok-tua's smoke test to be able to run `docker compose up -d <svc>`.
# GROK_TUA_SMOKE_COMPOSE=1
# AI_GATEWAY_DIR=/path/to/your/gateway/compose/project
